      "rev_n_1_787859_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_787859_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_787859",
        "thought_text": "Since N is very small (up to 11), the total number of permutations is at most 11!, which is around 39 million. However, the state space reachable via allowed swaps might be smaller. We can use Breadth-First Search (BFS) starting from the initial permutation to find the shortest path to the sorted state (1, 2, ..., N). BFS guarantees the minimum number of swaps.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794593746.0128245
      },
      "rev_n_2_198c49_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_198c49_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_198c49",
        "thought_text": "Alternatively, we can model this as a shortest path problem on a graph where nodes are permutations and edges are allowed swaps. Since edge weights are all 1 (one swap), BFS is optimal. We can implement this using a queue and a visited set to avoid cycles and redundant processing. Given the constraints, this should run within time limits if the reachable component isn't too large.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794593746.0128381
      },
      "rev_n_3_c9ddc7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_c9ddc7_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_c9ddc7",
        "thought_text": "Another approach could be Bidirectional BFS, searching simultaneously from the start permutation and the target permutation (sorted array) to meet in the middle. This could reduce the search space significantly if the diameter of the solution path is large, though for N=11, standard BFS might suffice. This strategy is more complex to implement but potentially faster for harder cases.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794593746.0128453
      },
      "rev_n_4_eb466a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_eb466a_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_eb466a",
        "thought_text": "Use Breadth-First Search (BFS) starting from the initial permutation, treating each state as a node and allowed swaps as edges, to guarantee finding the minimum number of swaps to reach the sorted state.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8170668112507628417",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794593775.9859405
      },
      "rev_n_5_41873a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_41873a_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_41873a",
        "thought_text": "Since the goal is fixed (sorted array), perform a Bidirectional BFS: expand simultaneously from the initial permutation and the target sorted permutation to meet in the middle, reducing the search space significantly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8170668112507628417",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794593775.98596
      },
      "rev_n_6_65e2c8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_65e2c8_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_65e2c8",
        "thought_text": "Model the problem as a shortest path on a graph where nodes are permutations and edges are valid swaps; apply Dijkstra's algorithm with a priority queue, though BFS is sufficient since all edge weights are 1.",
